{"id":"chatcmpl-abd704e7-1c84-487e-9dde-61d9acad1211","object":"chat.completion","created":1795229526,"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"","content":"```json\n{\n  \"highlights\": \"This task demonstrates how to resolve ambiguous spatial findings in medical imaging when only a single view is available. The key strategy is to combine direct image feature analysis (blunting of posterior costophrenic angle) with domain-specific conventions (standard lateral view positioning) and, when uncertainty remains, to consult a specialized expert tool. The final answer leverages both visual evidence and known radiographic conventions to disambiguate laterality.\",\n  \"patterns\": [\n    \"When a single-view image lacks explicit laterality markers, infer side from standard acquisition conventions (e.g., left side against detector in lateral views) rather than guessing.\",\n    \"Identify the primary abnormal feature first (e.g., blunted costophrenic angle), then use that feature's typical anatomical correlates to narrow the differential.\",\n    \"When visual evidence is insufficient to disambiguate between options, invoke a domain-specific expert tool to obtain a targeted interpretation before committing to an answer.\",\n    \"Prefer the option that is most consistent with both the observed feature and the standard positioning convention, especially when the finding is described as 'small' and thus subtle.\"\n  ],\n  \"playbooks\": {\n    \"Medical imaging interpretation\": \"Analyze the view type and standard patient positioning first; then localize the abnormality using anatomical landmarks; use expert consultation when laterality or subtle findings are ambiguous.\",\n    \"Single-view spatial reasoning\": \"Use acquisition conventions (detector side, patient orientation) as priors to resolve left/right ambiguity; cross-check with visible landmarks (gastric bubble, diaphragm height).\",\n    \"Ambiguity resolution under limited evidence\": \"When two options are plausible from the image alone, combine feature evidence with domain conventions and, if needed, call a specialized tool to break the tie.\"\n  },\n  \"checklists\": [\n    \"Confirm the imaging view and standard patient positioning convention before assigning laterality.\",\n    \"Verify that the identified abnormality (e.g., costophrenic blunting) is consistent with the chosen option and, if uncertain, consult an expert tool.\"\n  ]\n}\n```"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":945,"total_tokens":1370,"completion_tokens":425,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":945},"completion_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":425}}}